The manufactured pillars were tested in the three-point bending process for the energy absorbing (Fig.  8 a). The construction of the stand and the method of operation is the subject of a patent of the authors of the manuscript, because it allows for conducting crash tests of a single element of a car body (e.g. a pillar), simulating the conditions that prevail during a collision of the entire car body with a stationary obstacle, as in tests conducted by an independent assessment organization NCAP vehicle safety. Patent title: “A stand for testing the energy consumption of B-pillars of wheeled vehicles”, number: PL 241,285, publication date: 29.08.2022. The stand is built, among others: from two supports to which the head and foot of the pillar are attached, shock-absorbing springs, a beater attached to the guides, and C-sections constituting the frame of the structure and ensuring the stiffness of the entire system (Fig.  8 b). The use of springs with a given operating force ensures the stiffness of the entire structure, corresponding to the stiffness provided by mounting the post to the car body. The patented method saves time and resources because there is no need to destroy the entire vehicle. There is currently no single universal public standard dedicated specifically to testing complete B-pillars as separate components. In industrial practice, three-point bending or compression tests of B-pillars are usually internal procedures used by automotive companies during product development (correlate with full-vehicle side-impact or roof-crush requirements, such as FMVSS 214, UN R95, UN R135, or FMVSS 216a). The principle of comparison is based on the force–deflection curve recorded during the quasi-static bending test. The energy absorbed by the pillar is calculated as the area under this curve: 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
